## a b s t r a c t
The functionalization of metal–organic frameworks (MOFs) is imperative and challenging for the development of practical MOF-based materials. Herein, a magnetically functionalized $Z\mathrm{{r}}$ -MOF $(\mathrm{Fe}_{3}0_{4}@\mathrm{MOF}.$ 525) was synthesized via secondary-growth approach to obtain an easily-separated and recyclable adsorbent for the removal of pharmaceuticals (tetracycline (TC) and diclofenac sodium (DF)). After loading $\mathrm{Fe}_{3}0_{4}$ nanoparticles (NPs), due to the increase of micropore volume and specific surface area caused by defects, the adsorption performance of $\mathrm{Fe}_{3}{\mathsf{O}}_{4}@\mathrm{MOF}–525$ was improved. The kinetics could be described by the pseudo-second-order kinetic model. The different adsorption capacity and initial rate were attributed to the properties of the pharmaceuticals, including the molecular size and hydrophobicity/hydrophilicity. In isotherm experiments, the maximum adsorption capacities of DF and TC on $\mathrm{Fe}_{3}{\cal O}_{4}@\mathrm{MOF}–525$ calculated by Sips model reached 745 and $277\ \mathrm{mg}{\cdot}\mathbf{g}^{-1}$ , respectively. The thermodynamic studies indicated the adsorption was endothermic and spontaneous. The effect of pH suggested that electrostatic interaction, $\pi\cdot\pi$ interaction, anion- $\cdot\pi$ interaction, and H-bonding were possibly involved in the adsorption process. The adsorbent was separated by magnetic and regenerated. Washed with ethanol, $\mathrm{Fe}_{3}0_{4}@\mathrm{MOF}{-}525$ remained about $80\%$ adsorption capacity after four cycles. In-situ photoregeneration under visible-light irradiation was another attractive method, where $>95\%$ TC was degraded in $^{4\mathrm{h}}$ The reaction with scavengers revealed that $^10_{2}$ was the dominant reactive species in our system, indicating the occurrence of Type II photosensitization. The separability, excellent adsorption performance, and recyclability of $\mathrm{Fe}_{3}0_{4}@\mathrm{MOF}{-}525$ may lead to its beneficial applications in water treatment. $\circledcirc$ 2022 Elsevier Inc. All rights reserved.
## 1. Introduction
The excessive production and consumption of pharmaceuticals and personal care products (PPCPs) are causing a series of environmental issues. With the metabolism of human and animals, most of the pharmaceuticals are discharged into the natural aquatic environment by wastewater. The existence of PPCPs in surface water, groundwater and marine has already been reported [1,2]. The biological toxicity, carcinogenicity, and mutagenicity of PPCPs bring potential risks to aquatic organisms and human beings [3,4]. Notably, anti-inflammatory drugs and antibiotics are the two most frequently detected pharmaceuticals in the environment [5,6]. Diclofenac sodium (DF) is a commonly used anti-inflammatory drug for the treatment of inflammatory disorders or dysmenorrhea, but chronic exposure to DF may cause hemodynamic changes and thyroid tumors [7]. Tetracycline (TC) is a medicinal antibiotic, which has been widely applied for the treatment of animal diseases. After released into the environment, TC can induce bacterial resistance and disrupt the balance of flora [8,9]. Therefore, the removal of these emerging contaminants from water is of great significance.
Various technologies, such as microbial degradation, advanced oxidation, adsorption, membrane separation, and UV irradiation have been used to remove PPCPs [10–14]. As a low-cost highefficient purification method, adsorption is a promising technique for real-world applications [15,16]. Activated carbon and resin are two commonly used commercial adsorbents, yet their adsorption performance for pharmaceuticals is unsatisfactory [17,18]. Recently, metal–organic frameworks (MOFs), as a class of fascinating porous crystalline materials with regular network structure, have attracted a lot of attention. The large specific surface area, regular porous structure and versatile functionalization strategies make MOFs show great potential for adsorption and drug delivery [19,20]. For example, the storage and release of ibuprofen, aspirin and brimonidine by MOFs has been systematically studied [21,22]. The efficient removal of carbamazepine, chlorpromazine hydrochloride and sulfamethoxazole from water by MOFs has also been reported [23,24]. Therefore, water-stable MOFs are considered as excellent adsorbents for the removal of aqueous pharmaceuticals.
However, most of MOFs exist in the state of powder, which is dispersible in aqueous solution. As a result, even centrifuges can hardly separate MOF-base adsorbents thoroughly from the solution, which seriously affects their recyclability. For real-world applications, this is one of the most severe problems that MOFbase materials need to be solved. The magnetic functionalization is a facile strategy to obtain easily-separated and recyclable MOFs [25,26]. Due to the high saturation magnetization, low cost, and non-toxicity, $\mathrm{Fe}_{3}0_{4}$ magnetic nanoparticles (MNPs) have been used to form magnetic composites with MOFs. ZIF- $67@\mathsf{F e}_{3}0_{4}@\mathsf{E S M}$ was synthesized by ultrasound assistance and applied for the removal of organic dyes from water [27]. $\mathrm{Fe_{3}O_{4}@M I L-100(F e}$ ) was successfully prepared via an in-situ growth method and applied for photoFenton degradation of levofloxacin [28].
MOF-525 is one of the water-stable MOFs composed of $Z\mathrm{{r}}$ -oxide clusters connected by tetrakis(4-carboxyphenyl)porphyrin (TCPP). With high porosity and abundant active sites, it has been used for the adsorption of pollutants from water [24]. Moreover, it possesses photoreactivity due to the organic ligand TCPP [29,30]. With relatively long excited-state lifetime, TCPP can produce reactive species [31]. Especially, highly ordered framework prevents TCPP molecules from deactivation and aggregation, promoting the decomposition of the pollutants [32]. Therefore, the magnetic functionalization of MOF-525 may provide an efficient, recyclable, and photo-regenerable adsorbent.
In this study, $\mathrm{Fe}_{3}{\mathsf{O}}_{4}@\mathrm{MOF}–525$ was successfully synthesized by using the secondary-growth approach. To the best of our knowledge, this is the first report on the synthesis of magnetically functionalized MOF-525 and its applications in environmental remediation. Compared with the post-synthetic modification approach, secondary-growth approach protected $\mathrm{Fe}_{3}0_{4}$ nanoparticles from leaking. The increased surface area and porosity of $\mathrm{Fe}_{3}$ - ${0_{4}@\mathrm{MOF}{-525}}$ is beneficial for the adsorption of two target pharmaceuticals, DF and TC. The adsorption kinetics, isotherms, thermodynamics, and possible mechanisms of DF and TC on $\mathrm{Fe}_{3^{-}}$ ${0_{4}@\mathrm{MOF}{-525}}$ were studied. Moreover, besides regenerated by commonly used solvent, the efficiency of in-situ photoregeneration was also tested. The mechanism, advantages and disadvantages of in-situ photo-regeneration method were analyzed. Our work expands the diversity of recyclable MOFs and provides valuable insights into the development of MOF-adsorbents for the removal of emerging contaminants.
## 2. Materials and methods
## 2.1. Materials
Zirconyl chloride octahydrate $(\mathrm{ZrOCl}_{2}{\cdot}8\mathrm{H}_{2}0,$ $99\%$ ), tetrakis(4-car boxyphenyl)porphyrin (TCPP, $97\%$ ), ferroferric oxide $\mathrm{\Te}_{3}{\mathsf{O}}_{4}$ , $98\%$ 1 and diclofenac $(99\%)$ were purchased from Shanghai Aladdin Biotechnology Co., Ltd. (China). Tetracycline hydrochloride $(99\%)$ was obtained from Shanghai Macklin Biochemical Co., Ltd. (China). $\mathsf{N},N.$ -dimetylformanide (DMF, $99\%$ ), isopropyl alcohol (IPA, $99\%$ ) and EDTA-2Na $(98\%)$ were purchased from Sinopharm Chemical Reagent Co., Ltd. (China). p-Benzoquinone (BQ, $98\%$ was offered by Alfa Chemical Co., Ltd. (China). Furfuryl alcohol (FFA, $99\%$ was obtained from Adamas Reagent Co., Ltd. (China). HPLC grade methanol and acetonitrile $(99.9\%)$ were offered by Merck KGaA (Germany). All materials were used as supplied without further purification.
## 2.2. Preparation of MOFs
## 2.2.1. Preparation of MOF-525
The synthesis method of MOF-525 was based on the previous work [33]. $0.038\mathrm{g}\mathrm{Zr}0\mathrm{Cl}_{2}{\cdot}8\mathrm{H}_{2}0$ was dissolved in $10\mathrm{mL}$ DMF, added to a Pyrex vial and sonicated to uniform dispersion. Then $_{0.008\mathrm{~g~}}$ TCPP was charged into the vial and sonicated. After that, $2.5~\mathrm{mL}$ acetic acid was added in the mixture. The mixture was heated for $^{72\mathrm{~h~}}$ at $65^{\circ}\mathrm{{C}}$ . After cooling down to room temperature, the purple precipitate was separated and collected by centrifugation. At last, the sample was washed by acetone and dried in a vacuum oven.
## 2.2.2. Preparation of $F e_{3}O_{4}@M O F–525$
$\mathrm{Fe}_{3}0_{4}$ nanoparticles were coated on the surface of MOF-525 to form $\mathrm{Fe}_{3}{\sf O}_{4}@\mathrm{MOF}–525$ via secondary-growth approach. In short, $\mathbf{0.005\textrm{g}}$ MOF-525 and $0.004\textrm{g}\ F\mathbf{e}_{3}0_{4}$ were dissolved in $10\ \mathrm{mL}$
DMF and added to a Pyrex vial. The mixture was sonicated for 2 h to make it evenly dispersed. Then, repeated the above steps shown in Preparation of MOF-525 to obtain $\mathrm{Fe}_{3}{\mathsf{O}}_{4}@\mathrm{MOF}{-}525.$ .
## 2.3. Characterization
The morphology of the products was observed by a scanning electron microscopy (SEM, Quanta 250, USA). Powder $\mathsf{X}$ -ray diffraction (PXRD) patterns of various materials were recorded on an X-ray diffractometer (MiniFlex 600, Japan) at the range of $5^{\circ}$ - $80^{\circ}$ . The scanning step was $0.02^{\circ}$ and the scanning speed was $5^{\circ}$ - $\mathrm{min}^{-1}$ . The functional groups of the samples were studied by a Fourier Infrared (FT-IR) spectrometer with ATR module (AVAT-AR 360, USA). The ${\sf N}_{2}$ adsorption–desorption isotherms of different samples were measured at $77~\mathrm{K}$ on Micrometrics ASAP 2460 system (USA) to evaluate their specific surface area and porosity. Magnetic properties of $\mathrm{Fe}_{3}{\mathsf{O}}_{4}@\mathrm{MOF}–525$ were analyzed by a vibrating sample magnetometer (MPMS XL-7, USA). The surface charge of the samples at different pH was determined by a Zeta potential analyzer (NanoBrook Omni, USA).
## 2.4. Adsorption experiments
The adsorption kinetic experiments were performed with $50~\mathrm{mg}{\cdot}\mathrm{L}^{-1}$ DF and TC stock solutions. $5~\mathrm{mg}$ of the adsorbent was added to $50~\mathrm{mL}$ of DF or TC solution in a conical flask and placed in an orbital shaker (ZWY-200D, China) at $200\ \mathrm{rpm}$ and $298\mathrm{K}.$ Liquid samples were collected by syringes at specific time intervals and filtered through $0.22~{\upmu\mathrm{m}}$ nylon filters. In adsorption isotherm experiments, DF and TC solutions with the different initial concentrations at the range of $10\mathrm{mg}{\cdot}\mathrm{L}^{-1}$ to $200\mathrm{mg}{\cdot}\mathrm{L}^{-1}$ were used. The suspension with $5\mathrm{mg}$ of the adsorbent and $50~\mathrm{mL}$ of DF or TC solution were well-mixed in a shaker for $^{12\mathrm{~h~}}$ at $298\ \mathrm{K}.$ In thermodynamic studies, $5~\mathrm{mg}$ of the adsorbent was added to $50~\mathrm{mL}$ of DF or TC solution and reacted at different temperature (298, 308, 318, and 328 K). To investigate the effect of $\mathsf{p H}$ , the experiments were carried out in the $\mathsf{p H}$ range of $\mathsf{4.0{-}9.0}$ with a contact time of $^{12\mathrm{~h~}}$ . The pH of DF and TC stock solutions $(50\mathrm{mg}{\cdot}\mathrm{L}^{-1}),$ were adjusted with aqueous HCl $(0.10\mathrm{~M})$ or NaOH $(0.05{\mathrm{~M}})$ . All experiments were repeated three times.
## 2.5. Analytical methods
The concentrations of DF and TC in samples were measured by a high-pressure liquid chromatography (HPLC) with a UV–Vis detector (LC-20AD, USA) and an Eclipse Plus C18 column (Agilent Technologies, USA). The concentration of DF was determined at maximum absorption wavelength of $276\ \mathrm{nm}$ . The mobile phase was $80\%$ methanol and $20\%20\ \mathrm{mM\H_{3}P O_{4}}$ aqueous solution at a flow rate of $1.0\ \mathrm{mL}{\cdot}\mathrm{min}^{-1}$ . As for TC, the concentration was determined at maximum absorption wavelength of $360\mathrm{nm}$ . The mobile phase was $10\%$ methanol, $20\%$ acetonitrile, and $70\%10\ \mathrm{mM}$ oxalic acid aqueous solution at a flow rate of $1.0\ \mathrm{mL}{\cdot}\mathrm{min}^{-1}$ .
## 2.6. Regeneration
## 2.6.1. Eluent regeneration
$5~\mathrm{mg}$ of $\mathrm{Fe}_{3}0_{4}@\mathrm{MOF}{-}525\mathrm{~u~}$ as used for the adsorption of DF or TC solution $50~\mathrm{mL}$ , $50\ \mathrm{mg}.\mathrm{L}^{-1}$ ) conducted at $150~\mathrm{rpm}$ and $25\mathrm{{\circC}}$ in an orbital shaker. After $^{12\mathrm{~h~}}$ adsorption, $\mathrm{Fe}_{3}{\sf O}_{4}@\mathrm{MOF}{-}525$ was collected and taken out by magnet, added into flasks with $50~\mathrm{mL}$ eluent (methanol) for regeneration. Then the regenerated sample was dried in an oven and used for the next adsorption cycle.
## 2.6.2. In-situ photo-regeneration
To confirm the in-situ photo-regeneration ability of $\mathrm{Fe}_{3}{\mathsf{O}}_{4}@\cdot$ MOF-525, the photo-decomposition rate of pharmaceuticals in $\mathsf{F e}_{3}.$ - ${0_{4}@\mathrm{MOF}}-525$ /visible light irradiation system was tested. A Xenon lamp (300 W) with a $\lambda>420\mathrm{nm}$ cut off filter was used as the light source. 5 mg $\mathrm{Fe}_{3}{\sf O}_{4}@\mathrm{MOF}{-}525$ was added into $50~\mathrm{mL}$ of $30\ \mathrm{mg\cdotL^{-1}}$ DF or TC solution. After $^{5\mathrm{~h~}}$ adsorption to reach equilibrium, the experimental group was irradiated while the control group was kept in dark. At certain time intervals, solution samples were collected and filtrated by $0.22\upmu\mathrm{m}$ nylon syringe filter. After $^{4\mathrm{h}}$ visible light illumination, $\mathrm{Fe}_{3}{\sf O}_{4}@\mathrm{MOF}–525$ was magnetically separated and dried for the next test.
The reactive species generated during degradation process was detected by the addition of scavengers. $1\ \mathrm{mM}$ Furfuryl alcohol (FFA), isopropyl alcohol (IPA), p-benzoquinone (BQ), and EDTA2Na were used as the ${}^{1}0_{2}$ , OH, $\bullet0_{2}^{-}$ , and hole $(\boldsymbol{\mathrm{h}}^{+})$ scavengers, respectively. The dominant reactive species was further monitored by electron spin resonance (EPR) technique. $5~{\mathrm{mg}}~{\mathrm{Fe}}_{3}{\mathrm{O}}_{4}@{\mathrm{MOF}}{-}525$ was added to water containing $50~\mathrm{mM}$ TEMP, which is the spintrapping agent for ${}^{1}0_{2}$ .
## 3. Results and discussion
## 3.1. Characterization
$\mathrm{Fe}_{3}{\sf O}_{4}@\mathrm{MOF}–525$ was successfully prepared via the secondarygrowth approach (Scheme 1). During the sonication process, the dispersed $\mathrm{Fe}_{3}0_{4}$ particles were positively charged, with a zeta potential of about $+\ 3.4\ \mathrm{mV}$ , whereas MOF-525 showed a zeta potential value of $-17.3\mathrm{mV}$ indicating its negatively charged surface. The zeta potential results demonstrated that the hybrid structure between $\mathsf{F e}_{3}0_{4}$ and MOF-525 was formed through electrostatic self-assembly process. The as-synthesized MOF-525 crystals served as the core for seeded growth strategy with $\mathrm{Fe}_{3}0_{4}$ nanoparticles dispersed on the surface. Afterwards, Zr ions reacted with TCPP, and a thin layer of MOF-525 was grown on the surface of the $\mathrm{Fe}_{3}{\cal O}_{4}@\mathrm{MOF}–525$ . All these components (secondary-grown MOF-525 layer, $\mathrm{Fe}_{3}0_{4}$ particles and MOF-525 core) make up the magnetically functionalized $Z\mathrm{r}$ -MOF. Compared with the postsynthetic modification approach, secondary-growth approach is more efficient as it involves the incorporation of $\mathrm{Fe}_{3}0_{4}$ during the growth of MOF-525. Therefore, it avoids the size limitation on $\mathrm{Fe}_{3}0_{4}$ diffusion due to the small pores in MOFs. The simulated structure of MOF-525 in Scheme 1 shows its cubic structure. Each cube is constructed by eight corner-sharing $Z\mathrm{r}_{6}(0\mathrm{H})_{4}0_{4}$ clusters and six face-sharing TCPP, where each TCPP is coordinated with four $\mathrm{Zr_{6}(O H)_{4}O_{4}}$ . The pore diameter of MOF-525 is about $20{\mathring{\mathsf{A}}},$ which is much smaller than $\mathrm{Fe}_{3}0_{4}$ NPs with an average diameter of $300\ \mathring{\mathsf{A}}.$ As a result, once $\mathrm{Fe}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}$ is incorporated into MOF-525 framework, the nanopores can prevent its leaking, like a ‘‘shipin-a-bottle”. The mass ratio of $\mathsf{F e}_{3}0_{4}$ to MOF-525 in obtained sample was about 1:9.
PXRD analysis was conducted to investigate the interior crystal structure of MOF-525 and $\mathrm{Fe}_{3}{\sf O}_{4}@\mathrm{MOF}{-}525$ (Fig. 1). The main characteristic peaks of the as-synthesized MOF-525 are located at $20=$ $6.5^{\circ},7.8^{\circ},9.1^{\circ},$ $11.2^{\circ}$ and $13.6^{\circ}$ , corresponding to the (01 1), (11 1), (002), (112), and (122) planes of MOF-525, respectively [33]. After washed with water and organic solvent, the $\mathrm{Fe}_{3}{\mathsf{O}}_{4}@\mathrm{MOF}.$ 525 sample showed characteristic peaks of MOF-525 and $\mathrm{Fe}_{3}0_{4},$ implying the successful magnetic-functionalization. In the PXRD pattern of $\mathrm{Fe}_{3}{\sf O}_{4}@\mathrm{MOF}–525$ , the peaks at $35.5^{\circ}$ , $57.3^{\circ}$ , $62.9^{\circ}$ are identical to the (31 1), (51 1), and (40 0) planes of $\mathrm{Fe}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}$ , which is clearer in the pattern of pure $\mathrm{Fe}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}$ . Due to the lattice defects caused by the existence of $\mathsf{F e}_{3}0_{4}$ during the secondary growth of MOF-525, the diffraction peaks of MOF-525 in $\mathrm{Fe}_{3}{\mathsf{O}}_{4}@\mathrm{MOF}–525$ pattern are slightly weakened, which is consistent with previous research results [34]. Although the preparation of $\mathrm{Fe}_{3}{\mathsf{O}}_{4}@\mathrm{MOF-}$ 525 is simple and gentle $(65~\mathrm{^{\circ}C})$ , the obtained crystals are very stable. As illustrated in Fig. S1, $\mathrm{Fe}_{3}{\sf O}_{4}@\mathrm{MOF}{-}525$ shows good stability in neutral, acidic, and alkaline aqueous solutions. Moreover, in Fig. S2, the PXRD patterns of $\mathrm{Fe}_{3}{\sf O}_{4}@\mathrm{MOF}{-}525$ after adsorption or photoreaction is consistent with that of the as-synthesized sample, indicating that the structure remains unchanged.
The SEM images (Fig. 2a and b) show crystallites of MOF-525 and $\mathrm{Fe}_{3}{\mathsf{O}}_{4}@\mathrm{MOF}{-}525$ . MOF-525 has cubic morphology with a size of $300{-}400~\mathrm{\nm}$ and smooth surface. After the loading of $\mathrm{Fe}_{3}0_{4}$ and the secondary-growth, nanoparticles are dispersed on the surface of cubic crystals to form $\mathrm{Fe}_{3}{\sf O}_{4}@\mathrm{MOF}–525$ . The TEM images in (Fig. 2c and 2d) manifest $\mathrm{Fe}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}$ nanoparticles with a diameter of about $30\mathrm{nm}$ are loaded on the surface of MOF-525, and a thin layer MOF-525 with a thickness of about $8\ \mathrm{nm}$ is on the $\mathrm{Fe}_{3}{\mathsf{O}}_{4}@\mathrm{MOF-}$ 525, wrapping $\mathrm{Fe}_{3}0_{4}$ around the original MOF-525. The EDX elemental mapping of $\mathrm{Fe}_{3}{\sf O}_{4}@\mathrm{MOF}{-}525$ shown in (Fig. 2e) further indicates that $\mathrm{Fe}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}$ NPs are successfully deposited on the outer layer of $\mathrm{Fe}_{3}{\sf O}_{4}@\mathrm{MOF}–525$ under the mild conditions. In Fig. S3, the $\mathsf{N}_{2}$ adsorption–desorption isotherms of MOF-525 and $\mathrm{Fe}_{3}{\cal O}_{4}@.$ MOF-525 show characteristic Type I adsorption. After magnetic modification, compared with the parent MOF-525 $(236~\mathrm{m}^{2}{\cdot}\mathrm{g}^{-1})$ , the BET surface area of $\mathrm{Fe}_{3}{\sf O}_{4}@\mathrm{MOF}{-}525$ increased significantly $(427~\mathrm{m}^{2}{\cdot}\mathbf{g}^{-1})$ . Further, the pore size distribution of MOF-525 and $\mathrm{Fe}_{3}{\sf O}_{4}@\mathrm{MOF}{-}525$ are exhibited in Fig. S4. The results show that both MOF-525 and $\mathrm{Fe}_{3}{\sf O}_{4}@\mathrm{MOF}{-}525$ have the dominant micropores of 1.2 and $2.0\ \mathrm{nm}$ . The volume of micropores around $1.2\ \mathrm{nm}$ in $\mathrm{Fe}_{3}{\sf O}_{4}@\mathrm{MOF}–525$ has increased significantly. The total pore volume of $\mathrm{Fe}_{3}{\sf O}_{4}@\mathrm{MOF}–525$ is 1.7 times that of MOF-525 (Table S1). The increase in surface area and porosity is beneficial for adsorption. The magnetization hysteresis curve of $\mathrm{Fe}_{3}{\sf O}_{4}@\mathrm{MOF}{-}525$ is shown in Fig. 3. The $\mathrm{Fe}_{3}{\sf O}_{4}@\mathrm{MOF}{-}525$ exhibited apparent magnetic properties owing to the successful loading of $\mathrm{Fe}_{3}0_{4}$ . The saturation magnetization value of $\mathrm{Fe}_{3}0_{4}@\mathrm{MOF}{-}525$ is up to 7.48 emu $\mathbf{g}^{-1}$ . The excellent magnetic sensitivity allows it to be easily separated and collected by the magnet after dispersed in aqueous solution (image inserted in Fig. 3).
## 3.2. Adsorption kinetics
The adsorption kinetics of pharmaceuticals onto MOF-525 and $\mathrm{Fe}_{3}{\sf O}_{4}@\mathrm{MOF}–525$ are shown in Fig. 4. At the beginning, the adsorption capacity rises rapidly. However, with the increasing adsorption time, the available sites gradually decrease and ultimately equilibrium is achieved. The adsorption performance of $\mathrm{Fe}_{3}{\cal O}_{4}@.$ - MOF-525 is better than that of MOF-525. This phenomenon can be explained by the fact that the surface area and volume of micropores in $\mathrm{Fe}_{3}0_{4}@\mathrm{MOF}{-}525$ has increased after magnetic functionalization (Table S1). In addition, the introduction of $\mathsf{F e}_{3}0_{4}$ affected the secondary growth of MOF-525 and formed defects, thereby providing more abundant active sites. The adsorption of DF is basically completed within $30~\mathrm{min}$ while the adsorption of TC takes $120\ \mathrm{min}$ to reach equilibrium. $\mathrm{Fe}_{3}{\mathsf{O}}_{4}@\mathrm{MOF}–525$ possesses better adsorption capacity and higher adsorption rate for DF. The kinetic results were interpreted by three kinetic models (Fig. S5 and S6): pseudo-first-order kinetic model, pseudo-second-order kinetic model and Elovich model. The details of adsorption kinetic models are listed in S3. Adsorption kinetic models, supporting information.
According to the correlation factors $(\mathsf{R}^{2})$ in Table S3, the pseudosecond-order kinetic model is more suitable for describing the adsorption process than the pseudo-first-order kinetic model. This result indicates that the adsorption process of DF and TC on $\mathrm{Fe}_{3}$ - ${0_{4}@\mathrm{MOF}{-525}}$ was possibly affected by chemical interactions and the adsorption capacity was related to the number of available sites on the adsorbent [35]. Elovich model can be used to describe chemical adsorption and is suitable for adsorption systems with heterogeneous surfaces [36]. Both the adsorption kinetic curves of DF and TC fit the Elovich model well (Table S3). The high correlation factor $(\mathsf{R}^{2}>0.98)$ further indicates there might be chemical interactions between the adsorbates and $\mathrm{Fe}_{3}{\sf O}_{4}@\mathrm{MOF}–525$ [37].
Based on the pseudo-second-order kinetic model, the initial adsorption rate $(\nu_{0})$ of DF on $\mathrm{Fe}_{3}{\cal O}_{4}@\mathrm{MOF}–525$ is $91.4\mathrm{mg}{\cdot}\mathbf{g}^{-1}{\cdot}\mathrm{min}^{-1}$ which is 15.5 times that of TC (Fig. 4). Moreover, the equilibrium adsorption capacity of DF on $\mathrm{Fe}_{3}{\sf O}_{4}@\mathrm{MOF}{-}525$ $\mathrm{{'}q_{e}=311~m g{\cdot}g^{-1}}.$
is also greater than that of TC $\mathrm{(q_{e}=200\ m g{\cdot}g^{-1}}$ ). The differences in the adsorption kinetics of DF and TC on $\mathrm{Fe}_{3}{\sf O}_{4}@\mathrm{MOF}–525$ may be attributed to their properties. As shown in Fig. S7, the molecular volume of TC is greater than DF (molecular diameter of TC is about $1.40\mathrm{nm}$ ; while DF is about $0.96\mathrm{nm}^{\cdot}$ ). Thus, the diffusion resistance of DF is expected to be weaker than that of TC, which is conducive to the boundary diffusion and intra-particle diffusion in the adsorption process. On the contrary, owning to the larger size of TC molecules, it can easily block the micropores $(\sim1.2\ \mathrm{nm})$ and prevent the intra-particle diffusion of other TC molecules. In addition, the adsorption process can be affected by the hydrophobicity/ hydrophilicity of the adsorbents and the adsorbates. The $\mathsf{l o g\ K_{o w}}$ values of DF and TC are 4.02 and $-1.37$ , respectively (Table S2), indicating that DF is much more hydrophobic than TC. Meanwhile, the hydrophilicity/hydrophobicity of the adsorbent should also be examined. Due to an abundance of hydrophobic organic linkers (TCPP molecules) in MOF-525, hydrophobic interactions may play a considerable role in the DF adsorption, which also contributes to the high adsorption capacity of DF on $\mathrm{Fe}_{3}{\sf O}_{4}@\mathrm{MOF}–525$ .
## 3.3. Adsorption isotherms and thermodynamic studies
The adsorption isotherms of DF and TC on MOF-525 and $\mathrm{Fe}_{3}$ - ${0_{4}@\mathrm{MOF}{-525}}$ are shown in Fig. 5. The adsorption capacities of DF and TC on MOFs increase rapidly at relatively low initial concentrations and then level off at high initial concentrations. The high removal rate at low equilibrium concentrations demonstrates that $\mathrm{Fe}_{3}{\sf O}_{4}@\mathrm{MOF}–525$ is effective in removing pharmaceuticals from wastewater. The experimental data were fitted and analyzed by different isotherm models: Langmuir model, Freundlich model, Temkin model, and Sips model (Fig. S8 and S9). The expressions for the models are presented in S4. Adsorption isotherm models and the calculated parameters of models are given in Table S4.
The parameters obtained from the isotherm models are helpful to explain the adsorption mechanism. The Langmuir model is based on the assumption that a single layer of adsorbate covers the surface of the adsorbent and there is no interaction between adsorbate molecules. Freundlich model is an empirical model that can be applied to the adsorption with ununiform affinities over the heterogeneous surface [38,39]. Compared the values of ${\tt R}^{2}$ , Langmuir model describes the adsorption process of pharmaceuticals on $\mathrm{Fe}_{3}{\mathsf{O}}_{4}@\mathrm{MOF}–525$ better (Table S4). The Temkin model considers the weak interaction between adsorbent and adsorbate. The model assumes that the heat of adsorption of all molecules in the layer will decrease linearly, which leads to a uniform distribution of binding energy [40]. The Temkin constant $(\mathsf{b}_{\mathrm{{T}}})$ is related to the heat of adsorption. As the values of $\ensuremath{\mathbf{b}}_{\mathrm{T}}$ in this work are all higher than
$0.045\mathrm{kJ}{\cdot}\mathrm{mol}^{-1}$ , suggesting the weak interactions between pharmaceuticals and $\mathrm{Fe}_{3}{\sf O}_{4}@\mathrm{MOF}–525$ .
Sips isotherm is derived from Langmuir and Freundlich models, which circumvents the Freundlich model-related concentration rising limit and predicts as Langmuir model at high concentration [41]. As our adsorption system is complex, the Sips model, which considers more comprehensive conditions, is more suitable than the others in describing the adsorption of DF and TC on $\mathrm{Fe}_{3}{\cal O}_{4}@.$ MOF-525. In Fig. 5, the adsorption isotherm curves all correlate well with the Sips model $\mathrm{~\AA~}^{2}>0.98)$ . The maximum adsorption capacities of DF and TC obtained by Sips model are 745 and $277~\mathrm{{mg}}{\cdot}\mathbf{g}^{-1}$ , respectively. As shown in Table S5, compared with other studies, the theoretical maximum adsorption capacity of DF on $\mathrm{Fe}_{3}{\sf O}_{4}@\mathrm{MOF}{-}525$ is the highest among adsorbents reported so far, which is 2.3 and 11.7 times that of UiO-66 and activated carbon, respectively. The adsorption capacity of TC onto $\mathrm{Fe}_{3}{\cal O}_{4}@.$ MOF-525 is also higher than most of the reported materials, such as biochar, resin, and graphene oxide (Table S6). Therefore, with high adsorption capacity, easily-separated $\mathrm{Fe}_{3}{\cal O}_{4}@\mathrm{MOF}–525$ is a promising adsorbent for the removal of pharmaceuticals.
The changes in energy and entropy during the adsorption process can be calculated to understand whether the adsorption will occur spontaneously [42]. Herein, the thermodynamic parameters, including $\Delta{\sf H}^{0}$ , $\Delta S^{0}$ , and $\Delta G^{0}$ , were calculated according to the following equations:
$$
l n K_{0}=-~\frac{\Delta H^{0}}{R T}+\frac{\Delta S^{0}}{R}
$$
$$
\Delta G^{0}=\Delta H^{0}-T\Delta S^{0}
$$
The ${\Delta}{\mathrm{H}}^{0}$ and $\Delta S^{0}$ values are measured via the linear plot of $\mathrm{lnK}_{0}$ versus $1/\mathrm{T}$ , where ${\mathrm{K}}_{0}$ is adsorption equilibrium constant. The positive $\Delta\mathsf{H}^{0}$ value indicates the process is endothermic and increasing solution temperature can activate the adsorption sites. The positive $\Delta S^{0}$ value presents the enhanced randomness of the solid solution interface. The negative $\Delta G^{0}$ value indicates the process is spontaneous and favorable at high temperature [43].
To obtain the thermodynamic parameters, the effect of temperature on DF and TC adsorption by $\mathrm{Fe}_{3}{\sf O}_{4}@\mathrm{MOF}{-}525$ was investigated. Fig. 6 exhibits that the adsorption capacities of DF and TC on $\mathrm{Fe}_{3}{\sf O}_{4}@\mathrm{MOF}{-}525$ increase with the increasing temperature. Meanwhile, because the diffusion rate of adsorbates is controlled by the temperature, the equilibrium time of the adsorption is decreased as the temperature changes from $298\mathrm{~K~}$ to $328\ \mathrm{K}.$ The thermodynamic analysis results are shown in Table S7. The positive $\Delta\mathsf{H}^{0}$ and $\Delta S^{0}$ values indicate that both the adsorption of DF and TC on $\mathrm{Fe}_{3}{\sf O}_{4}@\mathrm{MOF}{-}525$ are endothermic processes in which entropy is increased, while the negative values of $\Delta G^{0}$ manifest the adsorption processes are spontaneous. At high temperature, the pharmaceutical molecules obtain sufficient energy to interact with active sites of the adsorbent, leading to higher adsorption capacity [44].
## 3.4. Possible adsorption mechanism
pH significantly influences the dominant form of the contaminants and further affects the electrostatic interaction between the adsorbate and the adsorbent. Therefore, the adsorption experiments with different pH can illustrate the role of electrostatic interaction in adsorption process. The effects of pH on DF and TC adsorption are shown in (Fig. 7a) and the Zeta potentials of $\mathsf{F e}_{3}0_{4},$ MOF-525 and $\mathrm{Fe}_{3}{\sf O}_{4}@\mathrm{MOF}–525$ are also measured in (Fig. 7b). As shown in (Fig. 7a), the adsorption capacities are weakened under alkaline conditions. At $\mathsf{p H}9$ , the removal percentages of TC and DF are reduced by $17\%$ and $30\%$ , respectively. Therefore, the adsorption performance of $\mathrm{Fe}_{3}{\sf O}_{4}@\mathrm{MOF}–525$ will be restricted by the pH of the solution.
The zero potential point $(\mathsf{p H}_{\mathsf{Z P C}})$ of MOF-525 is approximately 3.2, and there is little change after loading $\mathrm{Fe}_{3}0_{4}$ nanoparticles. When $\mathrm{pH}>\mathsf{p H}_{\mathrm{ZPC}},$ the surface of $\mathrm{Fe}_{3}{\sf O}_{4}@\mathrm{MOF}–525$ is negatively charged. The pKa of DF is approximately 4. Therefore, DF mainly exists in an anionic form when $\mathsf{p H}>4$ . Therefore, when the pH increases from 4 to 9, with the enhancement of the electrostatic repulsion, the adsorption capacity of DF on $\mathrm{Fe}_{3}{\sf O}_{4}@\mathrm{MOF}–525$ gradually decreases. TC is an amphoteric molecule with three dissociation constants. There are four main forms at different $\mathsf{p H}$ , including $\mathrm{TC^{+}}$ $\mathrm{|pH}<3.3\mathrm{\rangle}$ , ${\mathrm{TC}}^{0}$ $3.3<\mathrm{pH}<7.7\cdot$ ), $\mathsf{T C}^{-}$ $7.7<\mathsf{p H}<9.7;$ , and $\mathsf{T C}^{2-}$ $\mathrm{(pH>9.7)}$ [35]. When the solution pH increases, anions (TC-) gradually replace neutral molecules $(\mathsf{T C}^{0})$ to become the dominant form in the solution. Meanwhile, negative charge aggregates on the surface of $\mathrm{Fe}_{3}{\sf O}_{4}@\mathrm{MOF}–525$ . Therefore, it shows a similar decline in adsorption capacity as DF due to the electrostatic repulsion [45]. However, when pH is 9, $\mathrm{Fe}_{3}{\sf O}_{4}@\mathrm{MOF}–525$ still has certain adsorption capacities for TC and DF. Therefore, other interactions also play significant roles in the adsorption.
The $\pi{-}\pi$ interaction may be one of the reasons. The $\pi-\pi$ stacking between the aromatic rings of the pharmaceutical molecules and the porphyrin macrocycles/aromatic rings of $\mathrm{Fe}_{3}0_{4}@\mathrm{MOF}{-}525$ contributed to the adsorption under alkaline conditions [24]. In addition, $\pi{-}\pi$ electron-donor–acceptor interaction can occur between the conjugated enone structure of TC and $\mathfrak{p}$ -electrons in $\mathrm{Fe}_{3}{\cal O}_{4}@.$ MOF-525 [46]. The TC/DF anions can form anion- $\pi$ interaction with the aromatic rings in $\mathrm{Fe}_{3}{\sf O}_{4}@\mathrm{MOF}–525$ . Previous studies suggested that the H-bonding can play a role in the diffusion and adsorption processes [47]. H-bonding in our system probably involves: (i) the H of C-OH in TC/DF molecules as the H-donor and the O of $z\mathrm{r}{-}0{-}\mathsf{C}$ as the H-acceptor; (ii) Zr-OH groups of $\mathrm{Fe}_{3}0_{4}@\mathrm{MOF}–525$ as the Hdonor and the O of ${\mathsf{C}}{=}0/\mathsf{C}-0\mathsf{H}/\mathsf{C}-0$  structure in TC/DF as the H-acceptor; (iii) NH acting as both the H-donor and the Hacceptor. The adsorption mechanism of DF and TC on $\mathrm{Fe}_{3}0_{4}@-$ MOF-525 is shown in Fig. 8 and the plausible structure for the binding is shown in Table S8.
## 3.5. Regeneration
## 3.5.1. Eluent regeneration
The durability and recyclability are important for the real-world applications. The regeneration of adsorbents can recycle adsorbents and cut the cost in removal of contaminants. In this work, ethanol was used for the regeneration of $\mathrm{Fe}_{3}{\sf O}_{4}@\mathrm{MOF}–525$ as it has been commonly applied as eluent for the desorption of guest molecules from the pores of MOFs [24,48]. Fig. 9 illustrates the DF and TC adsorption capacity of spent $\mathrm{Fe}_{3}{\sf O}_{4}@\mathrm{MOF}–525$ after regeneration. In the regeneration process, the magnetically functionalized adsorbent was easily separated from the suspension by magnetic and washed with ethanol. After four cycles, $\mathrm{Fe}_{3}{\cal O}_{4}@.$ MOF-525 still maintains good adsorption capacities for both DF and TC, remaining $81\%$ and $80\%$ of its initial adsorption capacity (Fig. 9). The gradual loss of adsorption capacity was attributed to the strong interactions between a small amount of the pharmaceutical molecules and the adsorbent, which could not be eluted and occupied the binding sites. In addition, although eluent regeneration is a universal and simple regeneration method, the adsorbate is only transferred from the adsorbent to the regeneration solution, where the risk is not eliminated.
## 3.5.2. In-situ photo-regeneration
Since the organic ligand of MOF-525 is TCPP, it can produce singlet oxygen $({}^{1}0_{2})$ under irradiation [29,49]. Inspired by the previous reports that $^10_{2}$ can efficiently decompose tetracycline [50– 52], the in-situ photo-regeneration of $\mathrm{Fe}_{3}{\sf O}_{4}@\mathrm{MOF}{-}525$ after TC adsorption was investigated. As shown in (Fig. 10a), the degradation of TC under irradiation without a catalyst or with $\mathrm{Fe}_{3}0_{4}$ is negligible. Owing to the adsorption equilibrium, the concentration of TC with $\mathrm{Fe}_{3}0_{4}@\mathrm{MOF}{-}525$ in the dark remains unchanged. However, $>95\%$ of TC is degraded in $^\textrm{\scriptsize4h}$ with $\mathrm{Fe}_{3}{\sf O}_{4}@\mathrm{MOF}–525$ under the visible-light irradiation. Compared with other photocatalysts, such as $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ and Fe-MILs, $\mathrm{Fe}_{3}{\sf O}_{4}@\mathrm{MOF}{-}525$ exhibits higher TC removal efficiency. Notably, only $21\%$ of TC is degraded by pure TCPP powder. This phenomenon can be explained by the fact that TCPP molecules are easy to aggregate and become inactive in solution. The results indicate that $\mathrm{Fe}_{3}0_{4}@\mathrm{MOF}{-}525$ can eliminate TC and achieve in-situ regeneration. Therefore, the recycling performance of $\mathrm{Fe}_{3^{-}}$ ${0_{4}@\mathrm{MOF}{-525}}$ after photo-degradation was tested. The adsorbent was irradiated after adsorption and collected by magnet for the next cycle. As shown in (Fig. 10b), the removal percentage maintains $>94\%$ after four cycles, which is better than eluent regeneration. According to the previous researches, the removal percentage of TC is also higher than some photocatalysts (Table S9). The readsorption ability of $\mathrm{Fe}_{3}{\cal O}_{4}@\mathrm{MOF}–525$ after photo-regeneration was also tested. In Fig. S10, after four cycles, the equilibrium adsorption capacity of TC onto $\mathrm{Fe}_{3}0_{4}@\mathrm{MOF}{-}525$ decreased slightly. Therefore, the in-situ photo-regeneration method is efficient for $\mathrm{Fe}_{3}{\mathsf{O}}_{4}@\mathrm{MOF}{-}525$ after TC adsorption.
To confirm the generation of ${}^{1}0_{2}$ by $\mathrm{Fe}_{3}{\mathsf{O}}_{4}@\mathrm{MOF}–525$ , electron paramagnetic resonance (EPR) technique was used. As shown in (Fig. 11a), no EPR signal is observed when the experiment is performed in the dark, while the strong $1:1:1$ characteristic triplet signal is observed under visible light, indicating the existence of $^10_{2}$ in our system [53]. Moreover, scavengers were used to further ascertain the reactive species involved in the degradation process and their contributions. FFA, IPA, BQ and EDTA-2Na were used as the $^10_{2}$ , OH, $\bullet0_{2}^{-}$ , and $\boldsymbol{\mathrm{h}}^{+}$ scavengers, respectively. In (Fig. 11b), the degradation rate of TC decreases significantly (about $80\%$ )
when FFA is added into the reaction system, indicating that ${}^{1}0_{2}$ plays a dominant role in the degradation of TC [54]. The degradation rate of TC changes little with the addition of IPA, BQ or EDTA-2Na, suggesting that OH, $\bullet0_{2}^{-}$ , and $\boldsymbol{\mathrm{h}}^{+}$ are not the main reactive species in our system [55].
However, the degradation performance of DF is unsatisfactory (Fig. S11). It has been reported that the degradation rate of organic contaminants by $^10_{2}$ highly depends on the chemical structure [56]. In our system, TC is vulnerable to $^10_{2}$ , while DF is much more stable. Compared with two regeneration method, the eluent regeneration method has a wide range of applications, but it cannot eliminate the adsorbate completely; on the contrary, in-situ photo-regeneration is more effective and the contaminants can be decomposed, but the scope of application is limited and related to the properties of the adsorbate. It is necessary to select a suitable regeneration method based on the actual situation.
## 4. Conclusion
In summary, $\mathrm{Fe}_{3}0_{4}$ nanoparticles were successfully loaded onto the MOF-525 by secondary-growth approach to obtain a magnetically functionalized MOF-base adsorbent, $\mathrm{Fe}_{3}{\sf O}_{4}@\mathrm{MOF}–525$ . $\mathrm{Fe}_{3}$ - ${0_{4}@\mathrm{MOF}{-525}}$ can be easily separated and collected by magnetic. The increase of micropore volume and specific surface area due to the defects created by the introduction of $\mathrm{Fe}_{3}0_{4}$ enhanced the adsorption performance. The adsorption kinetics, isotherms, thermodynamics, and the effect of $\mathsf{p H}$ were investigated. The interface interactions between DF and TC molecules and $\mathrm{Fe}_{3}{\sf O}_{4}@\mathrm{MOF}{-}525$ includes electrostatic interaction, $\pi{-}\pi$ interaction, anion- $\cdot\pi$ interaction, and H-bonding. The calculated maximum adsorption capacity of DF and TC on $\mathrm{Fe}_{3}{\sf O}_{4}@\mathrm{MOF}–525$ was 745 and $277\mathrm{mg}{\cdot}\mathbf{g}^{-1}$ , respectively, much higher than most of the reported adsorbents [57–60]. Eluent regeneration is a universal method and $\mathrm{Fe}_{3}{\sf O}_{4}@\mathrm{MOF}{-}525$ remained about $80\%$ adsorption capacity after four cycles. In addition, the porphyrin ligand (TCPP) in $\mathrm{Fe}_{3}{\sf O}_{4}@\mathrm{MOF}–525$ could be excited by visible-light to generate singlet oxygen, which was effective for TC degradation and realized the in-situ photoregeneration of $\mathrm{Fe}_{3}{\cal O}_{4}@\mathrm{MOF}–525$ . After four cycles of in-situ photo-regeneration, it still shows high removal percentage for TC $(>94\%)$ , better than some photocatalysts [61–63]. Our strategy explores the functionalization and diversification of MOF-based materials and provides an efficient and recyclable adsorbent for the treatment of aqueous emerging contaminants. In future work, the relationship between photodegradation rate and the structure of contaminants should be investigated.
